  <section class="content">
    <article>
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>Most English-language writing about baijiu falls into two traps. Either it treats the category as a curiosity — a novelty to be survived, described in the language of dares — or it reproduces marketing copy from the largest brands and calls it a guide.</p>
      <p>Neither helps someone standing in a shop holding a bottle they cannot read. The useful questions are narrower and more practical: what aroma type is this, what will it taste like, is the price plausible, how do I serve it, and what would make me regret buying it?</p>
      <p>Baijiu365 is built around those questions. The <a href="/db/">database</a> covers 16 varieties across 6 aroma types and 8 regions, each with verified standard codes, ABV ranges, price bands, production details, and tasting notes. The guides are written to be used at a shelf or a dinner table, not admired as prose.</p>

      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>Every entry in the database is assembled from three kinds of source, and we try to be clear about which is which:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chinese national standards.</strong> The GB/T codes are the backbone. GB/T 10781.1 for strong aroma, 10781.2 for light, 10781.3 for rice, 26760 for sauce, 14867 for phoenix, 18356 for Moutai's geographical indication, and 20821/20822 for the liquid-fermented and blended categories. These define what a product legally is, and they are the most reliable facts on any label.</li>
        <li><strong>Physical labels.</strong> Where we have access to a bottle, we transcribe the back label — raw materials, standard code, licence number, producer, ABV — rather than relying on secondary descriptions. Our own product page's <a href="/jinlingnian-30/#label">label transcription</a> is an example of the standard we hold ourselves to.</li>
        <li><strong>Producer and industry material.</strong> Process descriptions, product lineups, and pricing come from producers and from published industry sources. These are attributed but treated as claims rather than facts, because producers have an interest in how their process is described.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Where we do not know something, we say so. Where a number is an estimate, we mark it as one. Where a claim is unverifiable — most vintage language in this category — we say that too.</p>

      <h2>How we judge a bottle</h2>
      <p>Three principles run through every page:</p>
      <h3>Aroma type before brand</h3>
      <p>A brand tells you who made a bottle. The aroma type tells you what it will taste like. Almost every beginner mistake in baijiu comes from choosing by fame rather than by style, which is why the database is indexed by aroma type first and why <a href="/types-of-baijiu/">the aroma-type guide</a> is the most important page on the site.</p>
      <h3>Price is not quality</h3>
      <p>In baijiu, price tracks gifting demand and scarcity at least as much as it tracks what is in the glass. A $40 bottle from a serious regional house frequently drinks better than a $200 gift box. We say this repeatedly because it is the most expensive lesson a new buyer can learn.</p>
      <h3>Context is part of the product</h3>
      <p>Baijiu was designed for food, for long meals, and for social ritual. Judging it neat, cold, and alone — the way one might judge a vodka — produces a verdict that is technically about the spirit and practically useless. Our serving guidance exists because the same bottle can read as harsh or remarkable depending on how it is poured.</p>

      <h2 id="the-character-on-our-mark">The character on our mark</h2>
      <p>Readers occasionally ask whether our mark is a typo — why 酉 rather than 酒?</p>
      <p>It is deliberate. <strong>酉</strong> (yǒu) is the ancient pictograph of a wine vessel: a jar with a narrow neck and a rounded body. It is the original character for wine, and 酒 (jiǔ) is simply 酉 with the water radical 氵 added in front of it. In the oracle-bone script the two are effectively the same image; the modern distinction between "vessel" and "liquid" came later.</p>
      <p>So 酉 is not a misspelling of 酒. It is the older form — the root that 酒 grew from, and the twelfth Earthly Branch, which is why it also appears in the traditional Chinese hour system. Using it as a mark is a way of pointing at the whole tradition rather than one word for it.</p>
      <p>That said, we know it reads as obscure to anyone who does not read Chinese. The short version: <em>酉 is the vessel; 酒 is what you pour into it.</em> Both are correct; one is older.</p>

      <h2>What we publish and what we do not</h2>
      <p>We publish buying guidance, aroma-type references, tasting frameworks, serving and pairing advice, and label-level data. We do not publish paid rankings, invented scores, or "best of" lists that exist to capture search traffic.</p>
      <p>Two specific refusals are worth stating plainly:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>We do not present marketing as review.</strong> When we describe a producer's process, it is attributed. When we recommend a bottle, we say why and for whom, and we name the trade-off.</li>
        <li><strong>We do not publish third-party documents.</strong> Business licences, invoices, and correspondence belong to the people who hold them. Product labels are different — they are printed on every bottle sold and are fair to quote.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Our own product</h2>
      <p>Baijiu365 also sells a house label, <a href="/jinlingnian-30/">Jinling Nian 30</a> — a 52% vol strong-aroma baijiu from Suqian, Jiangsu. We disclose this wherever it appears, because a site that reviews a category and also sells into it owes readers that clarity.</p>
      <p>The product page follows the same standard as the rest of the site: full label transcription including the standard code and licence number, an explicit statement of what the label does <em>not</em> claim, and an FAQ that answers the awkward question about the "30" in the name directly. If you want a bottle from a different producer, <a href="/db/">the database</a> lists fifteen of them.</p>

      <h2>Related projects</h2>
      <p>Baijiu365 is a baijiu-focused companion to <a href="https://www.chinatripbox.com/">ChinaTripBox</a>, which covers the practical side of travelling in China — payments, transport, city planning, and the everyday details that trip planning usually misses. The overlap is the table: if you are going to be at a Chinese banquet, you need both the etiquette and the bottle.</p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>If something here is wrong — a standard code, a price band, a process description, a translation — we want to know. Baijiu is a category where details matter and where English-language sources are thin, so errors are likely and corrections are valuable. <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>, and read our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for how we handle updates.</p>

